Description: Cross-GPU copy: map linear buffers instead of reading them back
 On a hybrid laptop with a monitor on the NVIDIA GPU, every buffer a client
 commits to a window there is copied through the CPU (3009). Measured on a
 ThinkPad P16 Gen 3 with two windows redrawing at about 30 fps each: 450 MB/s
 copied and about 4 s of every 10 s spent in glReadPixels (5.5 ms per 6 MB
 buffer) on the compositing thread.
 .
 A linear dma-buf can be mapped and copied with memcpy instead, which is
 several times cheaper. Clients only allocate linear buffers when nothing
 better is on offer, so MIR_DMABUF_LINEAR_ONLY=1 makes Mir advertise just
 the linear modifier (for formats that have one); umoja-mir sets it when a
 monitor is wired to the other GPU. Anything else still goes through
 glReadPixels as before.
 .
 Also: spare copy buffers are released when a buffer of a new size is
 needed, instead of up to eight (tens of megabytes each) being kept for
 good; the per-size statistics are only gathered in debug mode, and the
 debug line says how many copies were mapped.
Author: Umoja
Forwarded: no

